{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The role owning traversal, dispatch, actor invocation, process supervision, graph-call realisation, runtime facts, runtime event emission and replay, frames, continuations, retry and correction control, closure folds over runtime truth, re-entry after publish boundaries, lineage and provenance.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 591,
          "quote": "ABG owns traversal; dispatch; actor invocation; process supervision;\ngraph-call realisation; runtime facts; runtime event emission and replay;\nframes; continuations; retry and correction control; closure folds over\nruntime truth; re-entry after publish boundaries; lineage and provenance\n(P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F10.%20What%20an%20ODD%20product%20is%2FABG",
          "start_line": 587
        }
      ],
      "key": "abg",
      "label": "ABG"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The domain product owning domain asset meaning, graph-function meaning, the published function catalogue, policy overlays, domain query projections, and domain closure and gap interpretation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 597,
          "quote": "The ODD product owns domain asset meaning, graph-function meaning, the\npublished function catalogue, policy overlays, domain query projections,\nand closure and gap interpretation for its domain (P6, P1).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F10.%20What%20an%20ODD%20product%20is%2FODD%20product",
          "start_line": 595
        }
      ],
      "key": "odd_product",
      "label": "ODD product"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Service and query surfaces, including downstream read models, transport, sessions, routing, browser-safe projections and APIs. Their exposure of information supplies neither runtime nor continuation ownership.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 604,
          "quote": "Service or query layers may own transport, sessions, routing,\nbrowser-safe projection and API surfaces. They own neither runtime truth\nnor continuation truth. The real constructive carrier is never hidden\nbehind the word service (P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F10.%20What%20an%20ODD%20product%20is%2FQuery%20and%20service%20layers",
          "start_line": 601
        }
      ],
      "key": "service_query_layers",
      "label": "Service and query layers"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Explicit typed assets or nodes with typed domain boundaries; an asset that is a real produced or consumed boundary has a declared asset_surface.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 617,
          "quote": "Concrete assets must bind explicitly into\ntyped domain boundaries. Where an asset is a real produced or consumed boundary,\nits `asset_surface` is declared (P3).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.1%20Typed%20assets%20and%20nodes%20are%20explicit",
          "start_line": 615
        }
      ],
      "key": "typed_domain_assets",
      "label": "Typed domain assets and nodes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A named GraphFunction or lawful composition is the operative constructive carrier. Its public outer callable contract remains distinct from inner executable vectors.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 622,
          "quote": "Every operative constructive step is carried by one named `GraphFunction`\nor one lawful graph-function composition.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.2%20Graph%20functions%20are%20the%20primary%20constructive%20carrier",
          "start_line": 621
        }
      ],
      "key": "graph_function",
      "label": "GraphFunction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An outcome traversal declares governed movement before implementation: source and target asset types, edge traversal or graph-function name, closure or evaluation obligation, lineage obligation, ambiguity or gap obligation, and allowed F_D, F_P and F_H bindings. Its declarative carrier comprises GTL graph functions, typed nodes, vectors, operator and evaluator bindings, carrier schemas, policy, projection and lineage ledger declarations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 643,
          "quote": "An outcome traversal declares the governed movement before\nimplementation: source asset type; target asset type; edge traversal or\ngraph-function name; closure or evaluation obligation; lineage obligation;\nambiguity or gap obligation; allowed `F_D`, `F_P` and `F_H` bindings. The\ndeclarative carrier structure is GTL graph functions, typed nodes,\nvectors, operator bindings, evaluator bindings, carrier schemas, policy\ndeclarations, projection declarations and lineage ledger declarations\n(P0, P3).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.2A%20Outcome-first%20realisation%20order",
          "start_line": 636
        }
      ],
      "key": "outcome_traversal",
      "label": "Outcome traversal"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The GTL module is the operative publication surface for public graph-function carriers, traversed graphs, typed nodes and vectors, outer callable contracts and published refinement boundaries. The live function catalogue is machine-readable; the loop's ActionCatalog publishes lawful graph functions, vectors, targets, inputs and eligible outcomes for ABG consumption.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 658,
          "quote": "The live line must publish a machine-readable function catalogue. At\nminimum each retained function should expose its name, inputs, outputs,\nintent, and public or helper role.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.3%20The%20function%20catalogue%20is%20published",
          "start_line": 656
        },
        {
          "end_line": 666,
          "quote": "The product must publish the public graph-function carriers, the graph or\ngraphs those carriers traverse, typed node and vector surfaces, and the\nsemantic jobs or equivalent outer callable contracts bound to the public\ncarrier.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.4%20The%20GTL%20module%20is%20the%20operative%20publication%20surface",
          "start_line": 663
        },
        {
          "end_line": 848,
          "quote": "| `ActionCatalog` | Published lawful graph actions: graph functions, vectors, targets, inputs, and eligible outcomes. | GTL/product publication consumed by ABG. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 848
        }
      ],
      "key": "gtl_publication",
      "label": "GTL module and function catalogues"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The constructive evaluation or homeostasis loop runs from intent lineage and model synthesis through observation to the next lawful action. Its four distinct functions are synthesize_model, eval_gap, evaluate_next and evaluate_action. It corrects toward required conditions as observations arrive, without guaranteeing convergence or adequacy of those conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 796,
          "quote": "Every ODD product over GTL/ABG must preserve one constructive evaluation\nloop running from intent lineage and model synthesis through observation\nto the next lawful action. This is the profile's homeostasis loop, and\nits constitutional surface names the operative functions directly:\n`synthesize_model`, `eval_gap`, `evaluate_next` and `evaluate_action`\n(P2, P5, P6). The loop is this profile's binding of\n[ACTIVATION §1 Feedback and diagnosis](ACTIVATION.md#construction-relation):\nit corrects towards the required conditions as observations arrive,\nguarantees neither convergence nor the adequacy of those conditions, and\nits diagnoses distinguish the four causes named there (P2, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 787
        }
      ],
      "key": "constructive_evaluation_loop",
      "label": "Constructive evaluation loop"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "IntentLineage is admitted source intent and lineage references from human, imported source, prior product, intent engine or prior synthesized construction intent, grounded in ABG event-log truth and unable to invent graph actions. ProductAssetModel is desired and known typed asset truth synthesized from lineage, prior model and admitted product truth; its publication basis uses admitted events and product projection references, not runtime or action authority. Worksite is workspace, runtime archive, transform assets, product asset root, profile and observed file/process state, an observable substrate without authority by itself. RuntimeEventLog is append-only admitted runtime facts owned by ABG. RuntimeProjection is ABG replay-derived state over log and execution basis. ObservationSnapshot is a read-only runtime/worksite view, an ABG evaluator carrier with product domain rows. GapPressureRow is product meaning over admitted observation, describing missing, partial, blocked, waiting or ambiguous assets and evidence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 846,
          "quote": "| `ObservationSnapshot` | Read-only view of runtime truth plus worksite facts relevant to construction. | ABG evaluator carrier with product domain rows. |\n| `GapPressureRow` | Typed pressure over missing, partial, blocked, waiting, or ambiguous assets and evidence. | Product domain meaning over admitted observation. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 845
        },
        {
          "end_line": 844,
          "quote": "| `RuntimeEventLog` | Append-only admitted runtime facts. | ABG runtime truth. |\n| `RuntimeProjection` | Replay-derived current runtime state over event log and execution basis. | ABG projection truth. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 843
        }
      ],
      "key": "model_observation_carriers",
      "label": "Intent, model, worksite and observation carriers"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "TargetObligationBinding binds gap pressure exactly to the externally selected Product outcome, any admitted prerequisite or experiment basis, target assets, required roles, evidence references and admissible graph outcomes; it is product domain binding over GTL/ABG action authority. PriorityProjection deterministically ranks bound lawful actions under visible policy through ABG evaluator kernel and product policy. ConstructionIntent is the admitted selected action for one bounded graph invocation preserving the target binding's outcome and bounded-basis references, under ABG admission and traversal authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 847,
          "quote": "| `TargetObligationBinding` | Exact binding from gap pressure to the selected Product outcome, any admitted prerequisite or experiment basis, target assets, required roles, evidence refs, and admissible graph outcomes. | Product domain binding over GTL/ABG action authority. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 847
        },
        {
          "end_line": 851,
          "quote": "| `PriorityProjection` | Deterministic ranking over bound lawful actions under visible policy. | ABG evaluator kernel plus product policy. |\n| `ConstructionIntent` | Admitted selected action for one bounded graph invocation, preserving the selected outcome or admitted bounded-basis refs carried by its target binding. | ABG admission and traversal authority. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 850
        }
      ],
      "key": "target_and_intent_carriers",
      "label": "Target binding, priority and construction intent"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "AdmittedEvidence comprises admitted worker, process, product, execution, materialization, liveness and postflight evidence, under ABG/product admission rather than worker self-closure. EdgeFulfillmentLedger is closure evidence and convergence truth for one edge attempt/version, including counts, obligation rows, materialization, liveness and admission references and edge_converged. EdgeClosureDecision is the closed sum type close, yield, retry, repair, re-enter, reprice or block. NextActionBasis distinguishes initial_selection, post_yield_resume, post_close_graph_continuation, post_retry, post_repair, post_reenter, post_reprice and post_block; it supplies dispatch basis, not another controller. NextActionProjection observes this basis and current truth and selects a next graph action only when lawful, under ABG evaluation and product policy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 855,
          "quote": "| `AdmittedEvidence` | Admitted worker, process, product, execution, materialization, liveness, and postflight evidence. | ABG/product admission, not worker self-closure. |\n| `EdgeFulfillmentLedger` | Evidence and convergence carrier for one edge attempt or version. | Closure evidence truth. |\n| `EdgeClosureDecision` | Sum type over the ledger: close, yield, retry, repair, re-enter, reprice, block. | Closure decision truth. |\n| `NextActionProjection` | Read model that observes `NextActionBasis` plus current truth and selects a next graph action only when lawful. | ABG next-action evaluation projection with product policy. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 852
        },
        {
          "end_line": 849,
          "quote": "| `NextActionBasis` | Sum type naming why `evaluate_next` is being called: `initial_selection`, `post_yield_resume`, `post_close_graph_continuation`, `post_retry`, `post_repair`, `post_reenter`, `post_reprice`, or `post_block`. `post_reprice` records a re-entry request and carries no action-selection authority. | Dispatch basis for next-action evaluation. It is not a separate controller. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 849
        }
      ],
      "key": "evidence_decision_carriers",
      "label": "Evidence, ledger, closure and next-action carriers"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "synthesize_model takes IntentLineage, prior ProductAssetModel and admitted product truth to produce ProductAssetModel. eval_gap takes ProductAssetModel, RuntimeEventLog, RuntimeProjection and Worksite to produce ObservationSnapshot and GapPressureRow. evaluate_next takes NextActionBasis, fresh eval_gap, TargetObligationBinding, ActionCatalog and Policy to produce PriorityProjection, NextActionProjection and selected_action, plus a distinct core result when returning typed no-action. evaluate_action takes ConstructionIntent, AdmittedEvidence, TargetObligationBinding and Policy to produce EdgeFulfillmentLedger and EdgeClosureDecision. These are four authority functions even if libraries are shared.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 859,
          "quote": "The loop has one model-synthesis function and three distinct evaluation\nfunctions. They may share underlying libraries; they must not share\nauthority (P6):",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 857
        },
        {
          "end_line": 866,
          "quote": "| `evaluate_action` | `ConstructionIntent + AdmittedEvidence + TargetObligationBinding + Policy` | `EdgeFulfillmentLedger + EdgeClosureDecision` | Action-result evaluation. It decides close, yield, retry, repair, re-enter, reprice, or block for the action just taken. It does not choose the next graph action. |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 866
        }
      ],
      "key": "loop_functions",
      "label": "Model synthesis and three evaluation functions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Source-defined result domains distinguish edge-obligation evaluation, candidate availability, next-action selectability and candidate acceptance. Closure decisions and their cause refinements map to exactly one core value without adding a core value; their domain vocabulary and basis/reason references remain alongside that value. Invocational selection and ordinary empty selection after graph completion are not themselves core results. ACTIVATION dispositions accept, local_repair, re_enter and reject concern candidate acceptance and are not closure decisions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1134,
          "quote": "The closure decision is `evaluate_action`'s result over admitted\nevidence, mapped for the predicate \"the edge's obligations are fulfilled\non the exact basis\". The bounded action it judges has its own step result\nunder [ACTIVATION §3 Construction availability](ACTIVATION.md#construction-availability)\nfor \"a candidate is available\", and neither value counts toward the\nother's predicate\n([FOUNDATIONS §4 Conjunction of results](FOUNDATIONS.md#conjunction-of-results)).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.5D%20Constructive%20evaluation%20and%20yield%20loop",
          "start_line": 1128
        }
      ],
      "key": "loop_result_domains",
      "label": "Distinct loop result predicates"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Agentic conformance has an F_P walkthrough proving the constitutional process can be followed and an F_D deterministic artifact-admission gate. The agentic coder reasons, drafts and proposes from a minimal declared authority packet. The walkthrough covers real intake triage, smallest lawful re-entry, specification, requirement, design and ticket chain, constructive evaluation and closure proof.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1463,
          "quote": "Conformance has two halves: an F_P proof that the process can be followed,\nand an F_D gate that admits the produced artifacts (P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.7A%20Conforming%20agentic%20development",
          "start_line": 1462
        }
      ],
      "key": "agentic_conformance",
      "label": "Agentic conformance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Scenarios are graph-native proving surfaces that connect feature/use-case intent, initial assets, vectors, significant paths and closing evidence. Real authority-path negative proof is closure-grade evidence obtained by invalidating a real authority-bearing input and exercising the supported Product path, rather than fabricating the expected refusal.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1606,
          "quote": "Scenarios are graph-native proving surfaces, not only test\ndocumentation. A scenario bundle should make clear which feature or\nuse-case boundary is exercised; which assets are expected to exist before\nthe scenario starts; which vectors are expected to be traversed; which\nsignificant paths are expected to be exercised; and what evidence closes\nthe scenario (P2, P6).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F12.%20Scenario%20rule",
          "start_line": 1601
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1543,
          "quote": "Negative proof for an ODD or graph-native boundary exercises the supported\nProduct authority path. It must not manufacture the refusal state, blocked\nevent, gap or verdict that the system is supposed to derive (P6, P5;\nprovenance rc.2 #117b-real-authority-path-negative-proof-stdo-up-010).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F11.%20Core%20law%2F11.7B%20Real%20authority-path%20negative%20proof%20%28STDO-UP-010%29",
          "start_line": 1540
        }
      ],
      "key": "odd_proving_surfaces",
      "label": "Scenario and authority-path proving surfaces"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "ODD cold-start routes are ordered acquisition procedures for a concrete product. The profile supplies structural expectations, the product-specific method supplies domain law, and local product surfaces supply the current build line.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1717,
          "quote": "Starting cold in a concrete ODD product, read in the order below (P1, P2).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F18.%20Cold-start%20reading%20order",
          "start_line": 1717
        }
      ],
      "key": "odd_reading_routes",
      "label": "ODD cold-start reading routes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The canonical compression describes ODD as graph-native authoring of GTL/ABG domain products: explicit typed assets, named functions, published catalogue and module, ABG execution, one evaluation loop, history-derived views, manual explainability, outcome-first construction, lawful yield and packet-bounded agentic work with deterministic admission. It is a derived acquisition surface of same-digest sections §1, §2, §11.1–§11.5D, §11.7 and §11.7A and owns no determination.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1804,
          "quote": "This section is a compression of this member, a derived surface in the\nsense of [ACTIVATION §2](ACTIVATION.md#2-context-construction-under-an-envelope),\nAcquisition. Its sources are §1, §2, §11.1 to §11.5D, §11.7 and §11.7A of\nthis member at the same digest, and it owns no determination. Where it and\na source section differ, the source section decides",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F20.%20Canonical%20compression",
          "start_line": 1800
        }
      ],
      "key": "odd_canonical_compression",
      "label": "ODD canonical compression"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The ODD successor provenance register reports receipt of all 48 rc.2 ODD_METHOD rows under STDO-UP-015, with conserved modal force, exact formal tables/code and rewritten prose. It records successor member names, imperative-to-indicative restatement preserving force, labelled axiom paragraphs and British prose spelling with identifiers unchanged. It calls seven clauses new: bounded-step and refinement bindings, roles versus functor kinds, closure-result and §9 audit-result mappings, the foundation-family table, and compression's no-determination status. Its row for the constructive loop additionally identifies new no-action result distinctions and separation from ACTIVATION dispositions. Routes are provenance, not operative dependency.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1821,
          "quote": "Rows of rc.2 ODD_METHOD.md received by this member, all 48 under\nSTDO-UP-015. Anchors are into rc.2 ODD_METHOD.md; a locus is a section of\nthis file. Every conserved row is restated in this member's own text at\nthe predecessor's modal force; a route is provenance only. Formal content\n(code blocks and tables) is carried exactly and the prose around it is\nrewritten. Generalisations recorded here: rc.2 member file names are\nwritten as successor member names (ODD_METHOD as this profile, SPEC_METHOD,\nTICKETS, WORLD_MODEL, the software-design profile for DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD);\nimperatives (\"do not\", \"declare\") are written as indicative law or as\n\"never\", keeping their force; the predecessor's bold axiom titles are\nwritten as labelled paragraphs; American spellings are written British in\nprose and kept in identifiers (P6, P3).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/ODD.md#ODD%2F21.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 1810
        }
      ],
      "key": "odd_provenance_register",
      "label": "ODD source-disposition register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "POSTING_GUIDE owns the commentary layer: purpose, authority boundary, locations, identity, categories, structure, discussion state, posting rules and ratification. It consumes PRODUCT_DEFINITION's comment binding, TICKETS' work carriers, SPEC_METHOD's authority surfaces and EVALUATION's claims/evidence without defining them. Shared-method adoption is owned by SPEC_METHOD §6. Principle references P0–P6 and rc.2 routes identify stated grounds and provenance; the predecessor cut is not an application dependency.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
